<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hajo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hajo is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamrup">Kamrup</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>. It was the seat of the Koch Hajo kingdom from 1581. It is a place of pilgrimage for several faiths: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> revere the Hayagriva Madhava temple, some Buddhists believe it is where the Buddha attained nirvana, and Muslims visit the Poa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> mosque.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hajo.jpg" alt="Hajo" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jugal Bharali · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entrance_Madhab_mandir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
